e bishop (c8 later).  \n- Puts pressure on White’s pawn on c3 (might lead to c3-c4 later).  \n- Prepares possible queenside expansion and central control.  \n- Not as committal or weakening as f7-f5, which could allow White’s bishops to target our kingside more easily.  \n\n**Reason:**  \nWe need to activate our pieces and try to prevent White from castling into safety comfortably. Opening the c-file with c7-c5 pressures the queenside, potentially discouraging White from castling queenside, and prepares to bring our rooks into the game while keeping our kingside pawns solid for now.  \n\n**Answer:** P: c7-c5'}
